prose.text:
Driving a system with a periodic external force is a common way to maintain oscillations.
Whether steady oscillations can develop also depends on the system's response, the driving force, and energy dissipation;
a periodic force alone does not guarantee a constant amplitude.

**Oscillations that occur under the action of a periodic external force are called forced oscillations.**
Here, forced means that the motion is driven from outside;
it does not mean that the oscillating object cannot also have its own free oscillations.

An object or system that can respond to a periodic external force can undergo forced oscillations, as a periodically driven sieve does in a screening machine.
Its actual response also depends on its supports, constraints, inertia, and energy dissipation;
not every object subjected to any periodic force must oscillate in the same way or with the same amplitude.

